Chapter 1
Why Anyone Reads a Statement Twice
لماذا تُقرأ القائمة المالية مرتين
In Part 1 you prepared financial statements. You classified the accounts, you measured the inventory, you recognized the revenue, and at the end of Book 1 you had four statements for Orontes Foods, Inc. that balanced. That was the first reading of those statements: the reading that asks whether they are right.
Part 2 begins the second reading. It asks what the statements mean. Is Orontes able to pay its bills next month? Is it earning enough on what the owners have put in? Is it growing, and is the growth worth what it cost? Can it survive a bad year? These are different questions from the ones in Part 1, and they are answered with different tools: percentages, trends and ratios rather than debits and credits.
This chapter sets up the method and tells you where the numbers come from. Figure F501-01 is your map.
Figure F501-01. Chapter 1 roadmap: from a prepared statement to a judgment about the company.
