WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know what each statement contains, because you built them. You know that the balance sheet is at one date and the income statement is for a period, that net income flows into retained earnings, and that the ending cash on the statement of cash flows is the cash on the balance sheet. Part 2 never asks you to prepare those statements again. It asks you what to do with them once they are in front of you.
1.1  The second reading
A set of financial statements is a record of what happened. Reading it a second time turns that record into a judgment about what is likely to happen next. The two readings use the same paper and ask opposite questions. The first asks: does this follow the rules? The second asks: on this evidence, would I lend to this company, buy it, supply it, or work for it?
Nothing in the second reading is certain. A ratio is not a measurement the way a weight is a measurement. It is a comparison, and a comparison only means something against something else: the same company last year, a competitor, the industry, or a target the company set itself. A current ratio of 3.3 is neither good nor bad until you know what it was a year ago and what a food manufacturer normally carries.
That is the first rule of financial statement analysis, and it is the one candidates forget under exam pressure. A number alone answers nothing. Chapter 18 returns to it at the end, when you have all the ratios and have to produce a verdict.
EXAM TRAP   A ratio needs a comparison
A question that gives you one ratio and asks whether the company is healthy is testing whether you know that you cannot say.
When the exam asks for an interpretation, it will give you a comparison: a prior year, a competitor, or an industry figure.
Watch the direction of the comparison. A higher inventory turnover is usually better; a higher days-sales-outstanding is usually worse.
1.2  Who asks, and what each one wants
The same statements are read by people who want different things from them. The analysis follows the question, so the first thing to settle is whose question you are answering.
Figure F501-02 lists the readers you met in Part 1, with what each one looks for first.
Figure F501-02. Who reads the statements a second time, and what each looks for
@table
Reader | The question | Looks first at
A bank lending for 90 days | Will the cash be there when the note falls due? | Liquidity ratios and the cash cycle
A bank lending for ten years | Will the company still exist, and can it carry the interest? | Leverage and coverage ratios
A shareholder | Is the return on my money worth the risk? | ROE, margins, earnings per share
An acquirer | What is this business worth to me? | Cash flows, margins, asset turnover
A supplier granting credit | Will I be paid, and how slowly? | Days purchases in accounts payable
Management | Which part of the business is dragging? | Segment margins and asset turnover
@end
A lender and a shareholder can read the same balance sheet and reach opposite conclusions about the same fact. Heavy borrowing worries the lender, because it ranks ahead of him only in part and raises the chance of default. The same borrowing can please the shareholder, because interest is cheaper than equity and the surplus belongs to him. You will meet that disagreement again in Chapter 9, where capital structure is measured.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   The word "analysis"
In Arabic تحليل and in French analyse both cover the whole of this work. English divides the work into named techniques, and the exam uses the names: horizontal analysis, vertical analysis, ratio analysis, variation analysis. Learn the names as labels for procedures, not as synonyms for thinking about the numbers.
