SECTION CHECK   Test yourself
SC1-1  A bank is deciding whether to renew a 90-day note. Which group of ratios does it look at FIRST?
A.	Liquidity ratios
B.	Market ratios
C.	Profitability ratios
D.	Growth rates
SC1-2  A shareholder and a long-term lender read the same balance sheet. Which fact is most likely to please one and worry the other?
A.	A rise in cash
B.	A rise in borrowing
C.	A rise in inventory
D.	A rise in prepaid expenses
SC1-3  Which statement about a single ratio is correct?
A.	A current ratio above 2.0 shows the company is healthy.
B.	A ratio is a measurement, so it needs no comparison.
C.	A ratio means something only against a comparison.
D.	A ratio is reliable only if it is calculated from audited figures.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
1.3  The three lenses
There are only three things you can do to a financial statement to make it speak, and the first five chapters take them in turn.
The first is to turn every line into a percentage of one total. On the balance sheet every line becomes a percentage of total assets; on the income statement every line becomes a percentage of net sales. This is a common-size statement, and because the percentages run down the column it is also called vertical analysis. It lets you compare a company with one ten times its size, because both become 100 per cent. Chapter 2 does this to Orontes.
The second is to compare each line with itself in an earlier year. Every item is expressed against the same item in a base year, so you can see what has moved and by how much. Because the comparison runs across the page it is called horizontal analysis. Chapter 3 does this, and Chapter 4 turns the movements into growth rates.
The third is to divide one line by another. That is a ratio, and the rest of the book is ratios: twenty-five of them in Section A alone, grouped by what they are for. Liquidity ratios in Chapters 6 and 7, leverage in Chapters 8 to 10, activity in Chapters 11 and 12, profitability in Chapters 13 to 15, market ratios in Chapters 15 and 16.
Figure F501-03 sets the three side by side.
Figure F501-03. The three lenses, and what each one is for
@table
Lens | What it does | Also called | Answers
Common-size | Divides every line by one total | Vertical analysis | What is this company made of?
Trend | Divides every line by the same line in a base year | Horizontal analysis | What has changed?
Ratio | Divides one line by another | Ratio analysis | How do the parts relate?
@end
The three are not alternatives. An analyst uses all of them on the same statements, and each one raises the question the next one answers. Common-size analysis shows that cost of goods sold is 65 per cent of sales; trend analysis shows that it was 62 per cent two years ago; ratio analysis shows that inventory turnover fell at the same time, which suggests where the extra cost went.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: three lenses on one line
Step 1. Orontes's inventory at December 31, 2025 is $2,450,000, and total assets are $31,672,600. The common-size figure is 2,450,000 ÷ 31,672,600 = 7.74 per cent. Inventory is a small part of what Orontes owns.
Step 2. A year earlier, inventory was $2,300,000. The trend figure is 2,450,000 ÷ 2,300,000 = 1.065, so inventory rose by 6.5 per cent.
Step 3. Cost of goods sold for 2025 is $27,300,000. The ratio is 27,300,000 ÷ 2,450,000 = 11.14 times, so Orontes turns its inventory about eleven times a year.
Check. Each step used the same line and a different partner: a total, an earlier year, and another statement. The three answers are about size, about movement, and about speed, and none of them could be read off the balance sheet alone.
