1.5  Industry averages, and what they are worth
A company's own history is the easiest comparison, because you have it. The harder and more useful comparison is with other companies doing the same thing. For that you need industry figures, and those are published too.
Figure F501-05 names the usual sources.
Figure F501-05. Published sources of industry ratio averages
@table
Source | What it publishes
RMA Annual Statement Studies | Balance-sheet and income-statement ratios by industry, from bank lending files
Dun & Bradstreet Industry Norms and Key Business Ratios | Ratios by industry and by company size
Trade and industry associations | Operating statistics for their own members
Commercial data services | Company and industry ratios drawn from public filings
Government statistical agencies | Industry-level output, price and cost data
@end
An industry average is a comparison, not a target. Three things limit it, and the exam tests all three.
The first is that the industry classification may not fit. Orontes presses olive oil, mills tahini and bottles juice. A food manufacturing average covers companies that do none of those things, and a company that does all three sits in no single category.
The second is that the companies in the average may use different accounting. One uses LIFO and another FIFO; one leases its plant and another owns it. Part 1 taught you how far two sets of statements can differ while both follow the rules.
The third is that an average hides its own spread. Half the industry is on one side of it. A company at the average is not thereby safe, and a company away from the average is not thereby wrong: it may be better, or it may be doing something the average does not describe.
FALSE-FRIEND ALERT   Average, mean, median, norm
English "industry average" is loose: a published figure may be a mean, a median, or an upper and lower quartile. Arabic متوسط and French moyenne both suggest an arithmetic mean. Read the publication's own definition. Statement Studies, for example, reports quartiles as well as a median, and the quartile is often the more useful comparison.
TERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section
