1.6  The company every ratio is calculated from
Every ratio in Part 2 is calculated from one company, and it is the company you already know. Orontes Foods, Inc. presses olive oil and mills tahini in Amman, makes frozen pastries in Dubai, and sells through distributors across the Levant and the Gulf. Its three divisions are Oils, Tahini & Spreads, and Beverages. You prepared its statements in Part 1.
Figure F501-06 summarizes those statements. The full statements are reproduced in Chapter 2, and every ratio ahead is calculated from them, so any figure you do not recognize can be traced back to a line you wrote yourself.
Figure F501-06. Orontes Foods, Inc. — the three statements in summary (whole USD)
@table
Item | 2025 | 2024
Total current assets | 14,188,100 | 8,380,000
TOTAL ASSETS | 31,672,600 | 26,445,000
Total current liabilities | 4,280,000 | 3,570,000
Total liabilities | 11,279,625 | 10,070,000
Total stockholders' equity | 20,392,975 | 16,375,000
Net sales | 42,000,000 |
Cost of goods sold | (27,300,000) |
Operating income | 4,176,000 |
Net income | 2,969,100 |
Net cash provided by operating activities | 7,531,100 |
@end
Two features of the 2025 figures will occupy several chapters. Cash rose from $2,100,000 to $7,302,100, which is a very large movement for a company of this size and has to be explained before any liquidity ratio is believed. And operating income carries impairment losses of $2,724,000, which is a real charge but not one that recurs every year. Chapter 17, on the quality of earnings, is about exactly that kind of line.
The other companies of Part 1 come with it. Barada Wholesale, Inc. is the distributor whose own statements you worked on, and it supplies the case-style practice at the end of most chapters. Jordan Glass Co. is the bottle supplier Orontes holds an equity-method investment in. Levant Commerce Bank lends to Orontes, and Gulf Factor Co. buys its receivables.
EXAM TRAP   Analyze the company you are given
Exam questions give you the statements or the ratios they want used. Do not import what you think you know about the industry.
If a question gives both a prior year and an industry figure, the answer usually turns on one of them. Read what is asked.
