Chapter summary
Figure F501-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F501-07. Chapter 1 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Part 1 prepared the statements; Part 2 reads them a second time, to judge rather than to verify. A ratio is a comparison and says nothing on its own, so every figure is set against the prior year, a competitor or an industry figure. Three lenses do the work: common-size analysis divides every line by one total, trend analysis divides every line by itself in a base year, and ratio analysis divides one line by another. For a public company the evidence is published: the 10-K with its notes and MD&A, the 10-Q, the 8-K, the proxy statement and the company's own releases, all filed with the SEC and held in EDGAR. Industry averages are published too, by RMA, by Dun & Bradstreet and by trade associations, and all three of their limits are examinable: the classification may not fit, the accounting may differ, and the average hides its spread. Every ratio ahead is calculated from the Orontes statements you prepared in Part 1.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P1-01  Financial statement analysis differs from financial statement preparation mainly because analysis:
A.	uses only audited figures.
B.	asks what the statements mean rather than whether they are correct.
C.	is performed only by outsiders.
D.	requires no knowledge of accounting standards.
P1-02  Which document contains management's own explanation of why revenue moved during the year?
A.	The proxy statement
B.	Management's discussion and analysis in the Form 10-K
C.	The auditor's report
D.	Form 8-K
P1-03  An analyst needs quarterly revenue for the three months ended September 30. The BEST source is:
A.	the Form 10-K.
B.	the proxy statement.
C.	the Form 10-Q.
D.	the annual report to shareholders.
P1-04  Common-size analysis of an income statement expresses each line as a percentage of:
A.	total assets.
B.	net sales.
C.	net income.
D.	the same line in the base year.
P1-05  Use Figure F501-06. Orontes's total current liabilities as a percentage of total assets at December 31, 2025 is closest to:
A.	13.5 per cent
B.	21.0 per cent
C.	30.2 per cent
D.	35.6 per cent
P1-06  Which limitation of industry averages does an analyst meet when a company operates in three different product lines?
A.	The average may be out of date.
B.	No single industry classification describes the company.
C.	The average is a median rather than a mean.
D.	The companies in the average are audited by different firms.
P1-07  Two food manufacturers report inventory turnover of 11 times and 6 times. Which explanation must be ruled out before concluding that the first is better managed?
A.	The two use different inventory cost flow assumptions.
B.	The first has higher net income.
C.	The second pays higher dividends.
D.	The first is listed and the second is not.
P1-08  Which of the following is filed with the SEC when a company completes a major acquisition between its regular reports?
A.	Form 10-K
B.	Form 10-Q
C.	Form 8-K
D.	The proxy statement
P1-09  Use Figure F501-06. Orontes's working capital at December 31, 2024 (whole USD) is:
A.	4,810,000
B.	8,380,000
C.	3,570,000
D.	11,950,000
P1-10  A long-term lender and a shareholder disagree about a company's increased borrowing. The disagreement arises because:
A.	the lender uses different statements.
B.	borrowing raises the shareholder's return and the lender's risk.
C.	borrowing is reported differently under IFRS.
D.	the shareholder cannot see the debt.
Case-style practice: Barada Wholesale asks for a credit line
Barada Wholesale, Inc. distributes Orontes products across Jordan. It has asked Levant Commerce Bank for a one-year credit line of $600,000, to be drawn in the months when it builds stock before the autumn season. Barada is not a public company. It has offered the bank its last two years of statements, prepared by its own accountant and reviewed but not audited, together with a schedule of its receivables by age.
Required. Write a short note to the bank's credit officer setting out: (a) which of the three lenses you would apply first and why; (b) two comparisons you would want that Barada has not offered; and (c) one limitation of comparing Barada with a published wholesale-distribution average. Write in continuous prose, not in bullets, and keep it under 200 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC1-1  Answer A. A 90-day note is repaid out of short-term resources, so the bank looks at liquidity first.
B is wrong. Market ratios concern the share price and matter to investors, not to a short-term lender.
C is wrong. A profitable company can still be short of cash in a given quarter.
D is wrong. Growth rates describe direction over years, not the ability to pay in 90 days.
SC1-2  Answer B. Borrowing raises the shareholder's return when the company earns more than the interest, and raises the lender's risk of not being repaid.
A is wrong. A rise in cash reassures both.
C is wrong. A rise in inventory worries both, because it ties up cash and may not sell.
D is wrong. Prepaid expenses are small and interest neither party particularly.
SC1-3  Answer C. A ratio is a comparison; without something to compare it with it answers nothing.
A is wrong. There is no universal threshold; 2.0 may be high for one industry and low for another.
B is wrong. A ratio is a relationship between two amounts, not a measurement of a thing.
D is wrong. Audit improves the reliability of the inputs but does not remove the need for a comparison.
SC1-4  Answer B. On a common-size balance sheet every line is divided by total assets.
A is wrong. Net sales is the base for the common-size income statement.
C is wrong. Total equity is one of the lines being expressed, not the base.
D is wrong. Comparison with a base year is horizontal analysis.
SC1-5  Answer C. Common-size analysis runs down the column, so it is called vertical analysis.
A is wrong. Ratio analysis divides one line by another, not by a single total.
B is wrong. Horizontal analysis compares across years.
D is wrong. Variation analysis is percentage change over time, which is horizontal.
SC1-6  Answer B. Neither figure involves the income statement, so neither says how fast inventory moved.
A is wrong. The common-size figure of 7.74 per cent is exactly the size relative to the company.
C is wrong. The 6.5 per cent rise gives the direction.
D is wrong. The 6.5 per cent rise gives the size of the change.
SC1-7  Answer C. Officer and director compensation is disclosed in the proxy statement.
A is wrong. The 10-K carries the statements, notes and MD&A, not the detailed pay tables.
B is wrong. The 10-Q is a quarterly financial report.
D is wrong. The 8-K reports a single major event.
SC1-8  Answer B. A change of auditor is a reportable event, announced on a Form 8-K.
A is wrong. The 10-Q is a periodic financial report and may appear months later.
C is wrong. Waiting for the annual notes would leave users uninformed for most of a year.
D is wrong. The proxy statement concerns matters put to shareholders.
SC1-9  Answer B. The statements in a Form 10-Q are unaudited.
A is wrong. The annual statements in the 10-K are audited.
C is wrong. The comparatives presented in an audited annual report are covered by the audit.
D is wrong. The consolidated balance sheet in the annual report is the audited one.
SC1-10  Answer B. An industry average is a comparison whose classification, accounting basis and spread must be checked before it is relied on.
A is wrong. There is no reason a company should aim at the middle of its industry.
C is wrong. Industry averages are published statistics, not regulation.
D is wrong. The company's own past is a different comparison.
SC1-11  Answer C. A company in three product lines sits in no single published classification.
A is wrong. Audit status does not affect the choice of comparison.
B is wrong. Nothing in the question suggests unusual policies.
D is wrong. Published studies cover a range of sizes.
SC1-12  Answer B. Different cost flow assumptions change both inventory and cost of goods sold, so they change turnover without any difference in management.
A is wrong. Misstatement is possible but is not the first explanation to test.
C is wrong. Profitability does not determine turnover.
D is wrong. The question compares two companies, not either with an average.
SC1-13  Answer B. Using one familiar company means every ratio can be traced back to a line the reader prepared.
A is wrong. Ratios are routinely compared across companies.
C is wrong. Industry averages are published and are discussed in this chapter.
D is wrong. The exam uses many unrelated scenarios.
SC1-14  Answer B. A movement of that size must be explained before the closing figure is used; the statement of cash flows shows what produced it.
A is wrong. Comparing an unexplained figure with an average compounds the problem.
C is wrong. Recalculating uses the figure without understanding it.
D is wrong. Inflation adjustment is a separate issue and does not explain a one-year movement.
Multiple-choice questions
P1-01  Answer B. Preparation asks whether the statements follow the rules; analysis asks what they mean.
A is wrong. Analysts work on unaudited figures too, as in the Barada case.
C is wrong. Management analyses its own statements constantly.
D is wrong. Analysis requires more knowledge of the standards, not less, because the standards shape the numbers.
P1-02  Answer B. Management's discussion and analysis is management's own narrative explanation of the results.
A is wrong. The proxy statement concerns pay and matters put to a vote.
C is wrong. The auditor's report gives an opinion, not an explanation of performance.
D is wrong. An 8-K reports one event.
P1-03  Answer C. Quarterly figures are filed on Form 10-Q.
A is wrong. The 10-K is annual.
B is wrong. The proxy statement carries no quarterly results.
D is wrong. The annual report to shareholders is annual.
P1-04  Answer B. Every line of a common-size income statement is a percentage of net sales.
A is wrong. Total assets is the base for the balance sheet.
C is wrong. Net income is one of the lines expressed, not the base.
D is wrong. Comparison with a base year is horizontal analysis.
P1-05  Answer A. 4,280,000 ÷ 31,672,600 = 13.5 per cent.
B is wrong. This is roughly current liabilities over current assets inverted; it uses the wrong base.
C is wrong. This is close to total liabilities over total assets.
D is wrong. This is close to total liabilities over total assets plus an error.
P1-06  Answer B. Several product lines mean no single industry classification fits.
A is wrong. Currency of the data is a separate limitation.
C is wrong. Whether the statistic is a median or a mean is a definitional point, not the difficulty here.
D is wrong. The auditors of the comparison companies are irrelevant.
P1-07  Answer A. LIFO and FIFO produce different inventory and different cost of goods sold, so turnover differs without any difference in management.
B is wrong. Net income does not determine turnover.
C is wrong. Dividend policy is unrelated.
D is wrong. Listing status is unrelated.
P1-08  Answer C. A major acquisition between regular reports is announced on a Form 8-K.
A is wrong. The 10-K is annual.
B is wrong. The 10-Q is quarterly and may be months away.
D is wrong. The proxy statement concerns shareholder votes.
P1-09  Answer A. 8,380,000 − 3,570,000 = 4,810,000.
B is wrong. This is current assets alone.
C is wrong. This is current liabilities alone.
D is wrong. This adds the two instead of subtracting.
P1-10  Answer B. Borrowing raises the return on the shareholder's money when the company earns more than the interest, and raises the risk that the lender is not repaid.
A is wrong. Both read the same statements.
C is wrong. The disagreement is about economics, not presentation.
D is wrong. Debt is on the face of the balance sheet.
Case-style set: Barada Wholesale asks for a credit line
A good answer makes three points.
(a) Ratio analysis of liquidity comes first, because the bank's question is whether cash will be there within a year. The current and quick ratios, the cash cycle and the ageing schedule answer it directly; common-size and trend analysis are useful but secondary for a one-year facility.
(b) Two comparisons that are missing. The first is a forecast: the facility is to be drawn seasonally, so a cash forecast by month matters more than two completed years. The second is a comparison with other distributors of similar size, since Barada's own two years show direction but not whether the level is normal.
(c) One limitation. A published wholesale-distribution average is drawn mostly from audited companies using consistent policies; Barada's statements are reviewed rather than audited, so the inputs to its ratios are less reliable than the inputs to the average, and the comparison flatters or penalises it for reasons that have nothing to do with its business.
